        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We judged gutter sealants on the conditions that actually destroy them. A gutter joint sits in standing water after every rain, cycles between below freezing and well over 100 degrees in summer sun, and moves every time the metal expands and contracts. A sealant that looks fine on day one can pull away from the metal after a single winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adhesion to the right materials came first. We looked for products that list aluminum, galvanized steel, copper and vinyl as substrates, because many houses have a mix of original sections and later repairs. We gave extra credit to products that bond to damp surfaces, since gutter repairs are rarely done on a perfectly dry day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flexibility and temperature range came next. We checked each product's service range and how much movement it tolerates, and we favored tripolymer and synthetic rubber sealants that stay elastic over acrylics and hard caulks that crack. For more on why rigid products fail outdoors, see our explainer on <a href="/why-does-exterior-caulk-keep-cracking/">why exterior caulk keeps cracking</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consistency mattered too. A sealant that flows into a horizontal seam is ideal inside the trough, but the same product sags off a vertical downspout joint. We ranked products for the leak type they handle best rather than pretending one tube does everything equally well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we left out products that manufacturers say are not suited to permanent water contact. Some excellent polyurethane roof and flashing sealants carry that caveat, and a gutter seam sits in water after every storm, so they did not make the list despite their strength elsewhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leak-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Find Your Leak Type First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before you buy anything, figure out exactly where the water is coming from. Go out during a steady rain, or run a garden hose into the gutter at the high end, and watch the underside. Most gutter leaks fall into four categories, and each calls for a slightly different fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seam leaks happen where two sections of sectional gutter join, usually with a slip connector. The sealant between the lapped pieces has failed, and water wicks through the joint and drips from the bottom edge. These are the most common leaks on older sectional gutters and the easiest to fix with a flowing sealant like Geocel 2320. On seamless gutters there are no mid-run seams at all, so if you have seamless gutters, look at the corners and ends instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            End cap leaks show up as drips from the very end of a run. End caps are crimped onto the gutter and sealed from the inside, and when the sealant fails or the crimp loosens, water runs out around the edge. If the cap is loose, secure it with a couple of aluminum rivets before sealing; sealant is not a fastener.
          </p>

          <p>
            Downspout outlet leaks happen where the outlet tube passes through the bottom of the gutter. The flange around the hole was sealed when installed, and it often fails first because it sits at the lowest point where water pools. Leaks at downspout elbows and joints lower down are usually vertical joints and need a non-sag sealant.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, some apparent leaks are not leaks at all. Water pouring over the front edge usually means a clog or poor pitch, and water running behind the gutter onto the fascia often means missing drip edge. Sealant will not fix either.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HOSE TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Run water at the high end, watch the underside, and mark every drip with tape before you buy a single tube.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Five minutes with a garden hose tells you whether you have a seam, end cap, outlet or hole, and whether you have one leak or six. That decides which sealant to buy and how much.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

