        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Tripolymer vs Butyl vs Silicone
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gutter sealants come in a handful of chemistries, and the differences explain most early failures. For a gutter, you want a product that stays elastic, bonds to metal and vinyl, tolerates standing water and can be resealed over later.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Chemistry</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Damp Surfaces</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flexibility</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">UV Resistance</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict for Gutters</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tripolymer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
